  "question": {
    "text": "If $2 a+3 b+6 c=0$, then at least one root of the equation $a x^2+b x+c=0$ lies in the interval",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "shfGY",
      "text": "\n$(0,1)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "S9DhR",
      "text": "\n$(1,2)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "W2R1O",
      "text": "\n$(2,3)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "XYjvm",
      "text": "\n$(1,3)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let $f^{\\prime}(x)=a x^2+b x+c \\Rightarrow f(x)=\\frac{a x^3}{3}+\\frac{b x^2}{2}+c x+d$ $\\Rightarrow f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}\\left(2 a x^3+3 b x^2+6 c x+6 d\\right)$, Now $f(1)=f(0)=d$, then according to Rolle's theorem $\\Rightarrow f^{\\prime}(x)=a x^2+b x+c=0$ has at least one root in $(0,1)$",
    "image": null
  },
